Z||000008||Carrolles||01-07-2000||08:21 AM||||I found an 88 T-top coupe in a junk yard yesterday.  The t-tops are missing and the seal shows wear.  The windshield frame is bent on the driver's side.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Tom88gt||01-07-2000||12:22 PM||||Strip it for t-top parts.  Worn parts are better than no parts.  If everything else is there you can buy the glass (new) and probably the mechanisims too.  You just have to figure out how to convert a hard top car.  Make sure you have all the parts before you start.  Once you cut there is no going back (almost).<P>P seals.  I don't like the P seal design.  It looks unfinished.  When mine wear out I'm going to try some plastic door edge molding.  It seems the P-seals do nothing other than protect the bare glass edge.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Raydar||01-07-2000||04:58 PM||||Tom88... Was thinking along the same lines. <BR>Lose the P-seals and find another way to protect the edges and fill the gaps. Was thinking about running a 1/2" bead of black silicone around the very edge of the opening, placing wax paper over it, and latching the glass into place, until it cured. I like your idea even better. I can picture mine making a *huge* mess.||||reg||1
